What We Discussed and Worked On
Worked together on our first working version of the automated grading project for Professor Kodie McNamara from the Mathematics Department.
Created and reviewed the assignment_questions.txt file containing three example Python questions.
Worked on the student_answer_template.txt file to establish a consistent format for students to enter their student IDs, code answers, and explanations.
Set up the solution_key.json file to store the expected answers and point values for each question.
Developed and reviewed the grade_submissions.py program, which reads student submissions, extracts answers, checks formatting, and compares submitted code against the solution key.
Reviewed how the program handles missing answers, unknown question IDs, and submissions with incorrect formatting.
Examined the generated grades.csv file to see how the program records student answers, grading statuses, points earned, and instructor review notes.
Worked together to understand how the different files connect to form a complete grading process, from providing assignment questions to generating grading results.
Completed our first repository check-in oral exam, during which we demonstrated and discussed our project's current progress, explained the purpose of our files, and reviewed how our program processes student submissions.
Discussed the current limitations of our grading system, including its reliance on text comparison and the need for instructor review when submitted code does not match the expected answer exactly.
